{
 "version": 1,
 "id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000114",
 "title": "Nuclear Reactor",
 "created_at": "2026-03-15T00:00:00Z",
 "updated_at": "2026-07-12T00:00:00Z",
 "shared_cargo_toml": "[profile.release]\nopt-level = 1\nlto = false\ncodegen-units = 16",
 "cells": [
  {
   "id": "cell_intro",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "# Nuclear Reactor Neutron Diffusion\n\nThis notebook simulates a **2D cross-section of a nuclear reactor** using\none-group neutron diffusion theory with six groups of delayed-neutron\nprecursors, live in your browser: a Rust cell compiled to WebAssembly steps a\n300x300 flux grid **450 times per second** while you drive the control rods.\n\n## Physics\n\n**One-group neutron diffusion equation** (Stacey, *Nuclear Reactor Physics*, \u00a75.1):\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial \\phi}{\\partial t} = v \\left[ D \\nabla^2 \\phi - \\Sigma_a \\phi + (1 - \\beta)\\, \\nu \\Sigma_f \\phi + \\sum_{i=1}^{6} \\lambda_i C_i \\right] + S$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $\\phi(\\mathbf{r}, t)$: scalar neutron flux (neutrons / cm\u00b2 / s)\n- $v$: average neutron speed (cm / s)\n- $D$: diffusion coefficient (cm); how far neutrons scatter between collisions\n- $\\nabla^2 \\phi$: spatial Laplacian of the flux (leakage term)\n- $\\Sigma_a$: macroscopic absorption cross-section (1 / cm)\n- $\\nu \\Sigma_f$: fission neutron production rate (1 / cm); $\\nu$ is neutrons per fission\n- $\\beta = \\sum_i \\beta_i$: total delayed-neutron fraction (\u2248 0.0065 for ${}^{235}\\text{U}$)\n- $S$: external neutron source strength (neutrons / cm\u00b3 / s)\n\nRead the bracket as a ledger: diffusion spreads neutrons, absorption removes\nthem, prompt fission replaces $(1 - \\beta)$ of each generation immediately,\nand the $\\lambda_i C_i$ sum trickles in from precursor decay. That last term\nlooks like a rounding error. It is the entire reason this machine is operable.\n\n**Delayed-neutron precursor kinetics** (Keepin, *Physics of Nuclear Kinetics*, Ch. 4):\n\n$$\\frac{\\partial C_i}{\\partial t} = \\beta_i \\, \\nu \\Sigma_f \\, \\phi - \\lambda_i \\, C_i \\qquad (i = 1 \\ldots 6)$$\n\nwhere:\n\n- $C_i(\\mathbf{r}, t)$: concentration of delayed-neutron precursor group $i$\n- $\\beta_i$: delayed-neutron fraction for group $i$\n- $\\lambda_i$: decay constant for group $i$ (1 / s); how quickly precursors emit their delayed neutrons\n\nThe **effective multiplication factor** is computed from the material balance:\n\n$$k_\\text{eff} = \\frac{\\nu \\Sigma_f \\phi}{\\Sigma_a \\phi + \\text{leakage}}$$\n\nWhen $k_\\text{eff} = 1$ the reactor is **critical** (steady-state); $k < 1$ is\n**subcritical** (flux decays); $k > 1$ is **supercritical** (flux grows).\n\nBefore you drive the reactor below, it is worth understanding why it can be\ndriven at all: the next sections collapse the field equation to **point\nkinetics**, quantify the delayed-neutron timescale gap, and price reactivity\nthe way operators do, in dollars and cents.",
   "order": 0,
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Introduction"
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_pk_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## From a field to a point\n\nYou do not need the full PDE to understand reactor control. Integrate the flux\nover the whole core and what remains is **point kinetics**, the workhorse model\nof reactor dynamics:\n\n$$\\frac{dn}{dt} = \\frac{\\rho - \\beta}{\\Lambda}\\, n + \\sum_{i=1}^{6} \\lambda_i c_i \\qquad\\qquad \\frac{dc_i}{dt} = \\frac{\\beta_i}{\\Lambda}\\, n - \\lambda_i c_i$$\n\nNarrated plainly:\n\n- $n(t)$: total neutron population; $\\rho = (k - 1)/k$: **reactivity**, distance from perfect balance.\n- $\\Lambda$: **mean generation time**, birth to next fission. Roughly **25 microseconds** in a water-moderated core.\n- First term: the prompt ledger. Every $\\Lambda$ seconds the population multiplies by $(\\rho - \\beta)$ worth of imbalance.\n- Second term: delayed neutrons arriving from precursor decay; the $c_i$ equation is shelf bookkeeping (fission stocks the shelf, decay clears it).\n\nThat $(\\rho - \\beta)$ grouping is the most *load-bearing* notation in reactor\nphysics. While $\\rho < \\beta$, prompt neutrons **alone** form a dying chain;\nthe reactor grows only as fast as the precursor shelf restocks, in seconds.\nThe next cell shows the signature two-phase response: a fast **prompt jump**,\nthen a slow, steerable climb.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Point Kinetics",
   "order": 1
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_point_kinetics",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Point kinetics with U-235 six-group data: step +10 cents of\n// reactivity into a critical core and watch the two-phase response.\n\nconst BETA_I: [f64; 6] = [0.000215, 0.001424, 0.001274, 0.002568, 0.000748, 0.000273];\nconst LAMBDA_I: [f64; 6] = [0.0124, 0.0305, 0.111, 0.301, 1.14, 3.01];\nconst BETA: f64 = 0.0065;  // total delayed-neutron fraction\nconst GEN: f64 = 25e-6;    // prompt generation time (s)\n\nlet rho = 0.10 * BETA;     // +10 cents, far below prompt critical\nlet dt = 1e-4;\n\n// Start critical and in equilibrium: precursor production = decay.\nlet mut n = 1.0_f64;\nlet mut c: [f64; 6] = std::array::from_fn(|i| BETA_I[i] / (GEN * LAMBDA_I[i]));\n\nlet checkpoints = [0.01, 0.05, 0.5, 2.0, 10.0, 30.0];\nlet mut rows: Vec<Vec<String>> =\n    vec![vec![\"0.00\".into(), \"1.000\".into(), \"equilibrium\".into()]];\n\nlet mut t = 0.0_f64;\nfor &stop in &checkpoints {\n    while t < stop {\n        let delayed: f64 = (0..6).map(|i| LAMBDA_I[i] * c[i]).sum();\n        let dn = ((rho - BETA) / GEN) * n + delayed;\n        for i in 0..6 {\n            c[i] += (BETA_I[i] / GEN * n - LAMBDA_I[i] * c[i]) * dt;\n        }\n        n += dn * dt;\n        t += dt;\n    }\n    let phase = if t < 0.2 { \"prompt jump\" } else { \"delayed climb\" };\n    rows.push(vec![format!(\"{t:.2}\"), format!(\"{n:.3}\"), phase.into()]);\n}\n\nTable::new(vec![\"time (s)\", \"relative power\", \"phase\"], rows)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Prompt Jump",
   "order": 2
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_delayed_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## The 0.65% that makes reactors possible\n\nDelayed neutrons are not a correction term; they are **the** reason a fission\nreactor is a machine humans can operate. The numbers make the case:\n\n- A prompt neutron finishes its entire life in about **25 microseconds**.\n- A precursor sits on the shelf (half-lives 0.23 s to 55.9 s, weighted average about **13 seconds**) before releasing its neutron.\n- Fold that 0.65% into the average and the effective generation time stretches from 25 \u03bcs to roughly **0.085 s**: a factor of about **3,400**.\n\nFor a mental model: prompt-only at $k = 1.001$ has an e-folding period of\n$\\Lambda / \\delta k = 25$ **milliseconds**; power multiplies by\n$e^{40} \\approx 10^{17}$ every second. No mechanical system moves rods on that\ntimescale. With delayed neutrons pacing it, the same $k$ gives a period near\n**70 seconds**: steerable with an electric motor. The prompt-only case is not\nworth running live, so it stays in a fence:\n\n```rust\n// Prompt-only at k = 1.001: dn/dt = (0.001 / 25e-6) * n = 40 n.\n// n(t) = e^(40 t): about 5e8 by t = 0.5 s, f64 overflow by t = 17.7 s.\nlet mut n = 1.0_f64;\nloop { n *= 1.00004; } // 1 microsecond steps, hopeless within one frame\n```\n\nThe next cell computes these numbers straight from Keepin's six-group data.\nThey are directional rather than precise ($\\Lambda$ varies by core design),\nbut the ratio is the point.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Why Reactors Are Controllable",
   "order": 3
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_timescales",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Quantify the time cushion delayed neutrons buy, straight from\n// Keepin's six-group data for thermal fission of U-235.\n\nconst BETA_I: [f64; 6] = [0.000215, 0.001424, 0.001274, 0.002568, 0.000748, 0.000273];\nconst LAMBDA_I: [f64; 6] = [0.0124, 0.0305, 0.111, 0.301, 1.14, 3.01];\nconst BETA: f64 = 0.0065;\nconst GEN: f64 = 25e-6; // prompt generation time (s), typical water-moderated core\n\n// Abundance-weighted mean precursor lifetime: sum of (beta_i / beta) / lambda_i.\nlet mean_delay: f64 = (0..6).map(|i| (BETA_I[i] / BETA) / LAMBDA_I[i]).sum();\n\n// Effective generation time with delayed neutrons folded in.\nlet shelf: f64 = (0..6).map(|i| BETA_I[i] / LAMBDA_I[i]).sum();\nlet eff_gen = (1.0 - BETA) * GEN + shelf;\n\n// e-folding periods for a +100 pcm step (rho = 0.001).\nlet rho = 0.001;\nlet prompt_period = GEN / rho;\nlet delayed_period = (BETA - rho) * mean_delay / rho;\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"quantity\", \"value\"],\n    vec![\n        vec![\"prompt generation time\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.0} microseconds\", GEN * 1e6)],\n        vec![\"mean precursor delay\".to_string(), format!(\"{mean_delay:.1} s\")],\n        vec![\"effective generation time\".to_string(), format!(\"{eff_gen:.3} s ({:.0}x prompt)\", eff_gen / GEN)],\n        vec![\"period at +100 pcm, prompt only\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.0} ms\", prompt_period * 1e3)],\n        vec![\"period at +100 pcm, with delayed\".to_string(), format!(\"{delayed_period:.0} s\")],\n        vec![\"power gain in 1 s, prompt only\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.1e}\", (1.0_f64 / prompt_period).exp())],\n        vec![\"power gain in 1 s, with delayed\".to_string(), format!(\"{:.3}x\", (1.0_f64 / delayed_period).exp())],\n    ],\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Timescale Gap",
   "order": 4
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_dollars_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Reactivity in dollars and cents\n\nReactivity has natural units, and they are money. Define **one dollar**\n$= \\beta$: exactly the reactivity at which prompt neutrons alone sustain the\nchain. A hundredth of a dollar is a **cent**; small $\\rho$ is also quoted in\n**pcm** ($10^{-5}$). The ladder:\n\n- $\\rho < 0$: **subcritical**; the chain dies.\n- $0 < \\rho < \\beta$ (under one dollar): **delayed supercritical**. Precursors set the pace; the operator is in charge.\n- $\\rho \\geq \\beta$ (one dollar and up): **prompt critical**. The 3,400x cushion evaporates and power rises on the microsecond ledger. This is the cliff; SL-1 and Chernobyl both went over it.\n\nOperators quote rod moves in cents because the dollar scale pins the cliff at\na fixed place regardless of fuel; $\\beta$ is about 0.0065 for U-235 but only\n0.0021 for Pu-239, one reason plutonium-heavy cores handle more nervously.\nThe next cell converts $k_\\text{eff}$, the number on the live gauge below,\ninto pcm, dollars, and cents.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Dollars and Cents",
   "order": 5
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_dollars",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// The reactivity ladder: k_eff to pcm to dollars and cents.\n// One dollar = beta = 0.0065, the prompt-critical cliff.\n\nconst BETA: f64 = 0.0065;\n\nlet k_values = [0.980_f64, 0.995, 0.999, 1.000, 1.0002, 1.001, 1.0066];\n\nlet rows: Vec<Vec<String>> = k_values\n    .iter()\n    .map(|&k| {\n        let rho = (k - 1.0) / k;\n        let dollars = rho / BETA;\n        let regime = if dollars >= 1.0 {\n            \"PROMPT SUPERCRITICAL\"\n        } else if rho > 1e-9 {\n            \"delayed supercritical\"\n        } else if rho < -1e-9 {\n            \"subcritical\"\n        } else {\n            \"critical\"\n        };\n        vec![\n            format!(\"{k:.4}\"),\n            format!(\"{:+.0}\", rho * 1e5),\n            format!(\"{:+.2} $\", dollars),\n            format!(\"{:+.0} c\", dollars * 100.0),\n            regime.to_string(),\n        ]\n    })\n    .collect();\n\nTable::new(vec![\"k_eff\", \"reactivity (pcm)\", \"dollars\", \"cents\", \"regime\"], rows)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Reactivity Ladder",
   "order": 6
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_rod_worth_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## Control-rod worth\n\nA rod's **worth** is the reactivity change it causes, and it is nowhere near\nlinear in insertion depth. First-order perturbation theory says a small\nabsorber steals reactivity proportional to the **square of the flux** at its\nlocation (flux weights both the neutrons available and the importance of\nlosing them). With a roughly cosine axial flux:\n\n- **Differential worth** (reactivity per unit of travel) follows $\\sin^2(\\pi x)$: nearly zero at the ends, peaked at mid-core.\n- **Integral worth** follows the classic S-curve $W(x) = x - \\sin(2\\pi x)/(2\\pi)$.\n\nThe numbers are lopsided: the first 10% of insertion buys about **0.6%** of\ntotal worth; the middle 20% buys about **39%**. That is why fine power control\nhappens with rods near mid-core. Watch for this below: the $k_\\text{eff}$\ngauge barely reacts to the first slider increments and swings hardest through\nmid-range.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Control-Rod Worth",
   "order": 7
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_rod_worth",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "// Integral and differential rod worth for a rod sweeping a cosine\n// flux shape. Perturbation theory: local worth tracks phi^2 at the\n// tip, so differential worth goes as sin^2(pi x) and the integral\n// is the classic S-curve: W(x) = x - sin(2 pi x) / (2 pi).\n\nlet pi = std::f64::consts::PI;\nlet integral = |x: f64| x - (2.0 * pi * x).sin() / (2.0 * pi);\n\nlet mut rows: Vec<Vec<String>> = Vec::new();\nfor step in 0..=10_i32 {\n    let x = f64::from(step) / 10.0;\n    let differential = 2.0 * (pi * x).sin().powi(2); // normalised: mean = 1.0\n    rows.push(vec![\n        format!(\"{:.0}%\", x * 100.0),\n        format!(\"{differential:.2}\"),\n        format!(\"{:.1}%\", integral(x) * 100.0),\n    ]);\n}\n\nTable::new(\n    vec![\"insertion depth\", \"differential worth (x average)\", \"integral worth\"],\n    rows,\n)",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Rod Worth S-Curve",
   "order": 8
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_honest_md",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## What this simulation leaves out\n\nAn honest model states its own error bars. Four simplifications here matter in\na real core:\n\n- **One energy group.** Real neutrons are born near 2 MeV and thermalize to 0.025 eV; cross-sections swing by orders of magnitude along the way. Production codes use 2 to hundreds of groups.\n- **No xenon.** Xenon-135 is a fission-product poison with a capture cross-section near 2.6 million barns, building and decaying on a 6-to-9-hour timescale; it is why real reactors fall into a \"xenon pit\" after a power drop. Our clock runs for seconds; xenon never shows up.\n- **No thermal feedback.** Rising fuel temperature Doppler-broadens U-238 resonances and promptly pushes reactivity down; moderator density feedback does the same. Those negative coefficients make water reactors forgiving. Here the feedback loop is you and the sliders.\n- **Assorted pedagogy.** A 2D slice of a 3D core; neutron speed scaled from 2.2 \u00d7 10\u2075 cm/s to 50 cm/s so your eyes can follow the flux; explicit Euler; rod worths tuned for feel, not calibrated.\n\nNone of this changes the story: $\\beta$ is still 0.0065, precursors still take\nseconds, and the response to your rod moves is qualitatively the real thing.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "What We Simplify",
   "order": 9
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_orientation",
   "cell_type": "Markdown",
   "source": "## What You're Seeing\n\n| Element | Description |\n|---------|------------|\n| **Plasma core** | Fuel region; colour intensity shows neutron flux |\n| **Teal ring** | Water/graphite reflector surrounding the core |\n| **Silver bars** | Control rod blades (7 total); they absorb neutrons |\n| **Left gauge** | $k_\\text{eff}$ (white line = criticality at $k = 1$) |\n| **Right gauge** | Total neutron flux relative to peak |\n| **Bottom plots** | $k_\\text{eff}$ and total flux time histories |\n\n## Controls\n\nUse the sliders to adjust:\n- **Control Rod Depth**: how far the rods are inserted ($1.0$ = fully in)\n- **Rod Absorption ($\\Sigma_a$)**: neutron capture strength of the rod material\n- **Fission XS ($\\nu\\Sigma_f$)**: fission production rate of the fuel\n- **Fuel Absorption ($\\Sigma_a$)**: parasitic neutron capture in fuel\n- **Reflector Diffusion ($D$)**: how easily neutrons scatter through the reflector\n- **Reflector Absorption ($\\Sigma_a$)**: neutron capture rate in the reflector\n- **Neutron Source**: toggle the external startup source on/off\n\n> **Try it:** Set $k_\\text{eff} < 1$ by inserting the rods fully, then turn off\n> the neutron source and watch the flux decay exponentially to zero. Then pull\n> the rods out slowly and watch subcritical multiplication stretch the settling\n> time as $k$ creeps toward 1.",
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Reading the Simulation",
   "order": 10
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_controls",
   "label": "Reactor Controls",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "order": 11,
   "source": "// Interactive controls for the nuclear reactor simulation.\n// Values are automatically published to the simulation bus\n// so the reactor can read them each tick via `sim::read`.\n\n(\n    ui::slider(\"rod_depth\", 0.0, 1.0)\n        .step(0.01)\n        .label(\"Control Rod Depth\")\n        .default_value(0.50),\n    ui::slider(\"rod_absorption\", 0.1, 10.0)\n        .step(0.1)\n        .label(\"Rod Absorption (\u03a3\u2090)\")\n        .default_value(3.0),\n    ui::slider(\"fission_xs\", 0.01, 0.30)\n        .step(0.001)\n        .label(\"Fission XS (\u03bd\u03a3f)\")\n        .default_value(0.095),\n    ui::slider(\"fuel_absorption\", 0.01, 0.20)\n        .step(0.001)\n        .label(\"Fuel Absorption (\u03a3\u2090)\")\n        .default_value(0.082),\n    ui::slider(\"refl_diffusion\", 0.1, 3.0)\n        .step(0.01)\n        .label(\"Reflector Diffusion (D)\")\n        .default_value(1.50),\n    ui::slider(\"refl_absorption\", 0.001, 0.10)\n        .step(0.001)\n        .label(\"Reflector Absorption (\u03a3\u2090)\")\n        .default_value(0.005),\n    ui::checkbox(\"neutron_source\")\n        .label(\"Neutron Source\")\n        .default_value(false),\n)\n",
   "cargo_toml": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_0",
   "order": 12,
   "label": "Reactor Simulation",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "/// # 2D Nuclear Reactor Neutron Diffusion Simulation\n///\n/// Simulates a 2D cross-section of a cylindrical reactor with a\n/// withdrawable control rod.  The physics model uses one-group\n/// neutron diffusion with six groups of delayed-neutron precursors.\n///\n/// ## Physics\n///\n/// **One-group diffusion equation** (Stacey, *Nuclear Reactor Physics*, \u00a75.1):\n///\n///   \u2202\u03c6/\u2202t = v [ D \u2207\u00b2\u03c6 \u2212 \u03a3\u2090 \u03c6 + (1\u2212\u03b2) \u03bd \u03a3_f \u03c6 + \u03a3\u1d62 \u03bb\u1d62 C\u1d62 ] + S\n///\n/// **Precursor equations** (Keepin, *Physics of Nuclear Kinetics*, Ch. 4):\n///\n///   \u2202C\u1d62/\u2202t = \u03b2\u1d62 \u03bd \u03a3_f \u03c6 \u2212 \u03bb\u1d62 C\u1d62        (i = 1..6)\n///\n/// where:\n///   \u03c6  = scalar neutron flux\n///   v  = neutron speed (pedagogically scaled for visibility)\n///   D  = diffusion coefficient\n///   \u03a3\u2090 = macroscopic absorption cross section\n///   \u03bd\u03a3_f = macroscopic fission production rate\n///   \u03b2\u1d62, \u03bb\u1d62 = delayed-neutron fraction & decay constant for group i\n///   C\u1d62 = precursor concentration for group i\n///   S  = fixed external neutron source\n///\n/// ## Control Rod Model\n///\n/// A vertical absorber blade (B\u2084C / Ag-In-Cd) occupies a narrow channel\n/// at the core centre.  The rod inserts from the **top** of the core.\n/// Cross-section values inside the rod differ from the fuel:\n///\n///   - **Rod in**:  \u03a3\u2090 = 0.40 cm\u207b\u00b9, D = 0.40 cm  (strong absorber)\n///   - **Rod out**: \u03a3\u2090 = 0.02 cm\u207b\u00b9, D = 0.80 cm  (water channel)\n///\n/// The rod withdraws automatically until k_eff \u2265 1.0, which is tuned\n/// to occur at approximately 75 % rod withdrawal.\n///\n/// ## k_eff Gauge\n///\n/// A vertical colour-bar gauge on the left side of the canvas shows\n/// the effective multiplication factor k_eff in real time.  The gauge\n/// spans k \u2208 [0, 2] with a white marker at k = 1 (criticality).\n///\n/// ## Delayed-Neutron Data\n///\n/// Six-group parameters for thermal-neutron fission of U-235\n/// (Keepin, *Physics of Nuclear Kinetics*, Table 4-5):\n///\n///   | Group | \u03b2\u1d62       | \u03bb\u1d62 (s\u207b\u00b9)  | Half-life  |\n///   |-------|----------|-----------|------------|\n///   |   1   | 0.000215 | 0.0124    | 55.9 s     |\n///   |   2   | 0.001424 | 0.0305    | 22.7 s     |\n///   |   3   | 0.001274 | 0.111     |  6.2 s     |\n///   |   4   | 0.002568 | 0.301     |  2.3 s     |\n///   |   5   | 0.000748 | 1.14      |  0.61 s    |\n///   |   6   | 0.000273 | 3.01      |  0.23 s    |\n///\n/// Total \u03b2 = 0.0065 (effective delayed-neutron fraction for U-235).\n///\n/// ## References\n///\n///   - Stacey, W. M.  *Nuclear Reactor Physics*.  Wiley, 2nd ed., 2007.\n///   - Keepin, G. R.  *Physics of Nuclear Kinetics*.  Addison-Wesley, 1965.\n///   - Lamarsh, J. R. & Baratta, A. J.  *Introduction to Nuclear Engineering*.\n///     Prentice Hall, 4th ed., 2018.\n///   - Duderstadt, J. J. & Hamilton, L. J.  *Nuclear Reactor Analysis*.\n///     Wiley, 1976.\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Grid & geometry \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nconst N: usize = 300;        // grid cells per side\nconst CANVAS_W: u32 = 640;   // 20px k-gauge + 600px sim + 20px flux-gauge\nconst CANVAS_H: u32 = 600;   // simulation area is 600x600\nconst SIM_W: u32 = 600;\nconst GAUGE_W: u32 = 20;     // width of the k_eff gauge bar\nconst CENTER: usize = N / 2; // grid centre\nconst R_CORE: usize = 105;   // core radius in grid cells\nconst R_REFL: usize = 135;   // reflector outer radius\nconst ROD_HALF_W: usize = 6;  // control rod half-width in grid cells\n\n/// Seven control rod blade positions (x-coordinates), evenly distributed\n/// across the core.  Each rod is a vertical blade of width 2\u00d7ROD_HALF_W.\nconst ROD_POSITIONS: [usize; 7] = [\n    CENTER - 78, CENTER - 52, CENTER - 26, CENTER,\n    CENTER + 26, CENTER + 52, CENTER + 78,\n];\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Material properties \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n// (Duderstadt & Hamilton, Table 4.1, simplified one-group values)\n\nconst D_FUEL: f64 = 1.10;    // fuel diffusion coefficient (cm)\nconst D_REFL: f64 = 1.50;    // reflector diffusion coefficient\nconst D_ROD:  f64 = 0.20;    // control rod (B\u2084C / Ag-In-Cd), low diffusion\nconst D_WATER: f64 = 0.80;   // water channel (rod withdrawn)\n\nconst SA_REFL: f64 = 0.005;  // reflector absorption\nconst SA_WATER: f64 = 0.02;  // water channel absorption\n\n/// Void (vacuum): diffusion theory doesn't strictly apply, but we\n/// approximate free-streaming with a large D and tiny \u03a3\u2090 representing\n/// escape from the 2D simulation plane.\nconst D_VOID:  f64 = 1.8;    // CFL-safe: 4\u00b71.8\u00b750\u00b7dt = 0.80 < 1\nconst SA_VOID: f64 = 0.001;\n\n\n/// Pedagogically scaled neutron speed (cm/s).\n/// Real thermal neutrons travel at ~2.2 \u00d7 10\u2075 cm/s; we slow them\n/// so the simulation timescale is O(seconds) for visual clarity.\n/// (Lamarsh & Baratta, \u00a73.3: E = 0.0253 eV \u21d2 v = 2200 m/s)\nconst V: f64 = 50.0;\n\n/// Source position: placed off-centre to avoid the rod channel.\nconst SOURCE_X: usize = CENTER + 37;\nconst SOURCE_Y: usize = CENTER;\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Delayed neutron parameters (Keepin, Table 4-5, U-235 thermal) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nconst BETA: [f64; 6] = [\n    0.000215, 0.001424, 0.001274,\n    0.002568, 0.000748, 0.000273,\n];\nconst LAMBDA: [f64; 6] = [\n    0.0124, 0.0305, 0.111,\n    0.301,  1.14,   3.01,\n];\n/// Total delayed-neutron fraction \u03b2_total = \u03a3 \u03b2\u1d62 = 0.0065.\nconst BETA_TOTAL: f64 = 0.0065;\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Region classification \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nenum BaseRegion {\n    Fuel,\n    RodChannel,\n    Reflector,\n    Void,\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Simulation state \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nstruct ReactorSim {\n    /// Scalar neutron flux \u03c6(x,y).\n    phi: Vec<f64>,\n    /// Delayed-neutron precursor concentrations C\u1d62(x,y), for groups 1\u20136.\n    precursors: [Vec<f64>; 6],\n    /// Pre-classified base region for each cell (ignoring rod state).\n    region: Vec<BaseRegion>,\n    /// Control rod depth: 1.0 = fully inserted (from top), 0.0 = fully withdrawn.\n    rod_depth: f64,\n    /// Current k_eff computed from the material balance.\n    k_eff: f64,\n    /// Exponentially smoothed k_eff for stable display.\n    k_eff_smooth: f64,\n    /// Simulation time in seconds.\n    time: f64,\n    /// Frame counter for timing.\n    frame: u64,\n    /// Peak total flux seen so far (for gauge normalisation).\n    total_flux_peak: f64,\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Helpers \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n/// Distance from grid cell (i,j) to the core centre.\nfn dist(i: usize, j: usize) -> f64 {\n    let di = i as f64 - CENTER as f64;\n    let dj = j as f64 - CENTER as f64;\n    (di * di + dj * dj).sqrt()\n}\n\n/// Classify each cell into its base region (independent of rod position).\nfn classify_regions() -> Vec<BaseRegion> {\n    let mut region = vec![BaseRegion::Void; N * N];\n    for j in 0..N {\n        for i in 0..N {\n            let r = dist(i, j);\n            if r <= R_CORE as f64 {\n                // Inside core: check if this cell is in any rod channel.\n                let in_rod = ROD_POSITIONS.iter().any(|&cx| {\n                    (i as isize - cx as isize).unsigned_abs() < ROD_HALF_W\n                });\n                if in_rod {\n                    region[j * N + i] = BaseRegion::RodChannel;\n                } else {\n                    region[j * N + i] = BaseRegion::Fuel;\n                }\n            } else if r <= R_REFL as f64 {\n                region[j * N + i] = BaseRegion::Reflector;\n            }\n        }\n    }\n    region\n}\n\n/// Get the material properties (D, \u03a3\u2090, \u03bd\u03a3_f) for a cell, given rod state.\n///\n/// The rod inserts from the TOP of the core.  `rod_depth` \u2208 [0, 1]:\n///   - rod_depth = 1.0 \u2192 fully inserted (rod fills entire channel)\n///   - rod_depth = 0.0 \u2192 fully withdrawn (channel is all water)\n///\n/// The rod tip y-coordinate (in grid cells from the top of the core):\n///   rod_end_y = (CENTER \u2212 R_CORE) + rod_depth \u00d7 2 \u00d7 R_CORE\n///\n/// Grid cells in the rod channel with j < rod_end_y contain the absorber;\n/// those with j \u2265 rod_end_y contain water.\nfn material_at(\n    i: usize,\n    j: usize,\n    base: BaseRegion,\n    rod_depth: f64,\n    sa_rod: f64,\n    nusf: f64,\n    sa_fuel: f64,\n    d_refl: f64,\n    sa_refl: f64,\n) -> (f64, f64, f64) {\n    match base {\n        BaseRegion::Fuel      => (D_FUEL, sa_fuel, nusf),\n        BaseRegion::Reflector => (d_refl, sa_refl, 0.0),\n        BaseRegion::Void      => (D_VOID, SA_VOID, 0.0),\n        BaseRegion::RodChannel => {\n            // Rod tip position (grid row, measured from top of domain).\n            let core_top = CENTER as f64 - R_CORE as f64;\n            let rod_end_y = core_top + rod_depth * 2.0 * R_CORE as f64;\n            if (j as f64) < rod_end_y {\n                // Rod is present here (absorber).\n                (D_ROD, sa_rod, 0.0)\n            } else {\n                // Rod withdrawn from here (water channel).\n                (D_WATER, SA_WATER, 0.0)\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\n/// Compute k_eff from the current flux distribution using the material balance:\n///\n///   k_eff = \u03a3(\u03bd\u03a3_f \u00b7 \u03c6) / [ \u03a3(\u03a3\u2090 \u00b7 \u03c6) + leakage ]\n///\n/// where leakage \u2248 \u2212\u03a3(D \u00b7 \u2207\u00b2\u03c6) summed over all cells.\n/// (Stacey, \u00a75.3, Eq. 5.17, eigenvalue form of the diffusion equation)\nfn compute_k_eff(\n    phi: &[f64],\n    region: &[BaseRegion],\n    rod_depth: f64,\n    sa_rod: f64,\n    fission_xs: f64,\n    sa_fuel: f64,\n    d_refl: f64,\n    sa_refl: f64,\n) -> f64 {\n    let mut fission_sum = 0.0;\n    let mut absorption_sum = 0.0;\n    let mut leakage_sum = 0.0;\n\n    for j in 1..N - 1 {\n        for i in 1..N - 1 {\n            let idx = j * N + i;\n\n            let (d, sa, nusf) = material_at(i, j, region[idx], rod_depth, sa_rod, fission_xs, sa_fuel, d_refl, sa_refl);\n            let flux = phi[idx];\n\n            fission_sum += nusf * flux;\n            absorption_sum += sa * flux;\n\n            // Five-point Laplacian: \u2207\u00b2\u03c6 \u2248 \u03c6(i+1)+\u03c6(i\u22121)+\u03c6(j+1)+\u03c6(j\u22121) \u2212 4\u03c6\n            let lap = phi[idx + 1] + phi[idx - 1]\n                    + phi[(j + 1) * N + i] + phi[(j - 1) * N + i]\n                    - 4.0 * flux;\n            // Leakage = \u2212D \u2207\u00b2\u03c6 (positive = neutrons leaving the cell)\n            leakage_sum += -d * lap;\n        }\n    }\n\n    let loss = absorption_sum + leakage_sum;\n    if loss.abs() < 1e-30 {\n        0.0\n    } else {\n        fission_sum / loss\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Colour helpers \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\n/// Map a value t \u2208 [0, 1] to a \"hot plasma\" colour ramp.\nfn plasma(t: f64) -> (u8, u8, u8) {\n    let t = t.clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n    // Black \u2192 deep blue \u2192 purple \u2192 orange \u2192 bright yellow\n    let r = (1.5 * t - 0.2).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n    let g = (1.8 * t - 0.7).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n    let b = (0.5 + 1.5 * (0.5 - (t - 0.35).abs())).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n    (\n        (r * 255.0) as u8,\n        (g * 255.0) as u8,\n        (b * 255.0) as u8,\n    )\n}\n\n/// Colour for the k_eff gauge: blue(sub) \u2192 green(critical) \u2192 red(super).\nfn k_color(k: f64) -> (u8, u8, u8) {\n    if k < 1.0 {\n        // Blue \u2192 green as k goes 0 \u2192 1\n        let t = k.clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n        let r = 0;\n        let g = (t * 200.0) as u8;\n        let b = ((1.0 - t) * 200.0) as u8;\n        (r, g, b)\n    } else {\n        // Green \u2192 red as k goes 1 \u2192 2\n        let t = (k - 1.0).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n        let r = (t * 255.0) as u8;\n        let g = ((1.0 - t) * 200.0) as u8;\n        let b = 0;\n        (r, g, b)\n    }\n}\n\n// \u2500\u2500 Simulation trait impl \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n\nimpl Simulation for ReactorSim {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        let n2 = N * N;\n        let region = classify_regions();\n\n        // Seed the flux with a cosine-like profile in the fuel region\n        // so the fundamental eigenmode develops quickly.  Without this,\n        // the flux starts at zero and k_eff is meaningless for many frames.\n        let mut phi = vec![0.0; n2];\n        for j in 0..N {\n            for i in 0..N {\n                let idx = j * N + i;\n                if region[idx] == BaseRegion::Fuel {\n                    let r = dist(i, j) / R_CORE as f64;\n                    // Approximate J0 profile: peak at centre, zero at edge.\n                    phi[idx] = (1.0 - r * r).max(0.0) * 0.01;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        let precursors = std::array::from_fn(|_| vec![0.0; n2]);\n\n        ReactorSim {\n            phi,\n            precursors,\n            region,\n            rod_depth: 1.0,  // start fully inserted\n            k_eff: 0.0,\n            k_eff_smooth: 0.0,\n            time: 0.0,\n            frame: 0,\n            total_flux_peak: 1.0,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> Canvas {\n        // \u2500\u2500 Slider inputs \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        let rod_depth_val = sim::read::<f64>(\"rod_depth\").unwrap_or(0.50);\n        let rod_absorption = sim::read::<f64>(\"rod_absorption\").unwrap_or(3.0);\n        let fission_xs = sim::read::<f64>(\"fission_xs\").unwrap_or(0.095);\n        let fuel_absorption = sim::read::<f64>(\"fuel_absorption\").unwrap_or(0.082);\n        let neutron_source = sim::read::<f64>(\"neutron_source\").map(|v| v > 0.5).unwrap_or(false);\n        let refl_diffusion = sim::read::<f64>(\"refl_diffusion\").unwrap_or(1.50);\n        let refl_absorption = sim::read::<f64>(\"refl_absorption\").unwrap_or(0.005);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Time stepping \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        // CFL stability for the 2D explicit Euler diffusion scheme:\n        //   4 \u00b7 D_max \u00b7 v \u00b7 dt  <  1       (2D, 5-point stencil)\n        // D_max = 1.5 (reflector), v = 50 \u2192 dt < 1/300 \u2248 0.00333\n        // 15 sub-steps at 30 fps \u2192 dt = 1/450 \u2248 0.00222\n        // Check: 4 \u00b7 1.5 \u00b7 50 \u00b7 0.00222 = 0.667 < 1  \u2713\n        let dt: f64 = 1.0 / (30.0 * 15.0);\n        let sub_steps = 15;\n\n        self.time += 1.0 / 30.0;\n        self.rod_depth = rod_depth_val;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Diffusion sub-steps \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        for _ in 0..sub_steps {\n            let phi_old = self.phi.clone();\n\n            for j in 1..N - 1 {\n                for i in 1..N - 1 {\n                    let idx = j * N + i;\n                    let base = self.region[idx];\n\n                    let (d, sa, nusf) = material_at(\n                        i, j, base, self.rod_depth,\n                        rod_absorption, fission_xs, fuel_absorption,\n                        refl_diffusion, refl_absorption,\n                    );\n\n                    // Five-point Laplacian \u2207\u00b2\u03c6 (unit grid spacing).\n                    let lap = phi_old[idx + 1] + phi_old[idx - 1]\n                            + phi_old[(j + 1) * N + i]\n                            + phi_old[(j - 1) * N + i]\n                            - 4.0 * phi_old[idx];\n\n                    // Sum of delayed-neutron precursor decay: \u03a3 \u03bb\u1d62 C\u1d62.\n                    let delayed_src: f64 = (0..6)\n                        .map(|g| LAMBDA[g] * self.precursors[g][idx])\n                        .sum();\n\n                    // External source (localised off-centre).\n                    let src_strength = if neutron_source { 1.0 } else { 0.0 };\n                    let src = if i == SOURCE_X && j == SOURCE_Y {\n                        src_strength\n                    } else {\n                        0.0\n                    };\n\n                    // Diffusion equation (explicit Euler):\n                    //   d\u03c6/dt = v [ D\u2207\u00b2\u03c6 \u2212 \u03a3\u2090 \u03c6 + (1\u2212\u03b2) \u03bd\u03a3_f \u03c6 + \u03a3 \u03bb\u1d62C\u1d62 ] + S\n                    let dphi = V * (\n                        d * lap\n                        - sa * phi_old[idx]\n                        + (1.0 - BETA_TOTAL) * nusf * phi_old[idx]\n                        + delayed_src\n                    ) + src;\n\n                    self.phi[idx] = (phi_old[idx] + dphi * dt).max(0.0);\n\n                    // Update precursor concentrations (explicit Euler):\n                    //   dC\u1d62/dt = \u03b2\u1d62 \u03bd\u03a3_f \u03c6 \u2212 \u03bb\u1d62 C\u1d62\n                    for g in 0..6 {\n                        let dc = BETA[g] * nusf * phi_old[idx]\n                               - LAMBDA[g] * self.precursors[g][idx];\n                        self.precursors[g][idx] =\n                            (self.precursors[g][idx] + dc * dt).max(0.0);\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        }\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Compute k_eff \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        // Raw k_eff from material balance (noisy during early transient).\n        self.k_eff = compute_k_eff(&self.phi, &self.region, self.rod_depth, rod_absorption, fission_xs, fuel_absorption, refl_diffusion, refl_absorption);\n        // Exponential moving average (\u03b1 = 0.05) for a stable display.\n        self.k_eff_smooth = 0.95 * self.k_eff_smooth + 0.05 * self.k_eff;\n        sim::emit(\"k_eff\", &self.k_eff_smooth);\n        self.frame += 1;\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Fixed absolute colour scale \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        // Absolute log bounds so colours don't rescale dynamically.\n        // Range: 1e-10 (background) to 10.0 (bright critical flux).\n        const LOG_FLOOR: f64 = -23.0;  // ln(1e-10)\n        const LOG_CEIL:  f64 =   2.3;  // ln(10)\n        let log_range = LOG_CEIL - LOG_FLOOR;\n        let log_min = LOG_FLOOR;\n\n        // Total flux for the right-side gauge.\n        let total_flux: f64 = self.phi.iter().sum();\n        self.total_flux_peak = total_flux.max(self.total_flux_peak * 0.999);\n        sim::emit(\"total_flux\", &total_flux);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Render canvas \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        // Left 20 px: k_eff gauge.  Right 400 px: 200\u00d7200 grid \u2192 2\u00d7 scale.\n\n        let rod_depth = self.rod_depth;\n        let k_eff = self.k_eff_smooth;\n        let phi = &self.phi;\n        let region = &self.region;\n        let total_flux = total_flux;\n        let total_flux_peak = self.total_flux_peak;\n\n        Canvas::from_fn(CANVAS_W, CANVAS_H, |px, py| {\n            // \u2500\u2500 k_eff gauge (left strip) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            if px < GAUGE_W {\n                // Gauge maps k \u2208 [0, 2] to the full canvas height.\n                // Bottom of canvas = k=0, top = k=2.\n                let k_at_row = 2.0 * (1.0 - py as f64 / CANVAS_H as f64);\n\n                // Criticality marker (k = 1.0): white horizontal line.\n                let k1_y = (CANVAS_H as f64 * 0.5) as u32; // k=1 is at 50%\n                if py >= k1_y.saturating_sub(1) && py <= k1_y + 1 {\n                    return (255, 255, 255);\n                }\n\n                // Current k_eff needle: bright white line.\n                let k_y = ((1.0 - k_eff / 2.0) * CANVAS_H as f64) as u32;\n                if py >= k_y.saturating_sub(2) && py <= k_y + 2 {\n                    return (255, 255, 255);\n                }\n\n                // Filled gauge up to current k_eff.\n                if k_at_row <= k_eff {\n                    // Narrow the bar slightly for aesthetics (2px border).\n                    if px >= 2 && px < GAUGE_W - 2 {\n                        return k_color(k_at_row);\n                    }\n                }\n\n                // Dark background for gauge.\n                return (20, 20, 25);\n            }\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Total flux gauge (right strip) \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            if px >= GAUGE_W + SIM_W {\n                let frac = if total_flux_peak > 0.0 {\n                    (total_flux / total_flux_peak).clamp(0.0, 1.0)\n                } else {\n                    0.0\n                };\n                // Fill from bottom up.\n                let fill_y = ((1.0 - frac) * CANVAS_H as f64) as u32;\n                if py >= fill_y {\n                    let gx = px - (GAUGE_W + SIM_W);\n                    if gx >= 2 && gx < GAUGE_W - 2 {\n                        // Cyan-to-white gradient.\n                        let t = frac;\n                        let r = (50.0 + t * 205.0) as u8;\n                        let g = (180.0 + t * 75.0) as u8;\n                        let b = 255_u8;\n                        return (r, g, b);\n                    }\n                }\n                return (20, 20, 25);\n            }\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Simulation area \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            let sim_x = px - GAUGE_W;\n            let i = (sim_x / 2) as usize;\n            let j = (py / 2) as usize;\n\n            if i >= N || j >= N {\n                return (0, 0, 0);\n            }\n\n            let idx = j * N + i;\n            let base = region[idx];\n\n            match base {\n                BaseRegion::Void => {\n                    // Dim warm glow for leaked neutrons in void.\n                    let flux = phi[idx];\n                    if flux > 1e-20 {\n                        let t = ((flux.ln() - log_min) / log_range)\n                            .clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n                        let v = (t * 50.0) as u8;\n                        (v, v / 2, v / 4)\n                    } else {\n                        (10, 10, 15)\n                    }\n                }\n                BaseRegion::Reflector => {\n                    // Teal tint for reflector (water/graphite).\n                    let flux = phi[idx];\n                    let t = if flux > 0.0 {\n                        ((flux.ln() - log_min) / log_range).clamp(0.0, 1.0)\n                    } else {\n                        0.0\n                    };\n                    let b = (25.0 + t * 70.0) as u8;\n                    (b / 6, b / 2, b)\n                }\n                BaseRegion::Fuel => {\n                    let flux = phi[idx];\n                    let t = if flux > 0.0 {\n                        ((flux.ln() - log_min) / log_range).clamp(0.0, 1.0)\n                    } else {\n                        0.0\n                    };\n                    plasma(t)\n                }\n                BaseRegion::RodChannel => {\n                    // Determine if rod or water at this position.\n                    let core_top = CENTER as f64 - R_CORE as f64;\n                    let rod_end = core_top + rod_depth * 2.0 * R_CORE as f64;\n                    if (j as f64) < rod_end {\n                        // Rod present: silver-red tint (Ag-In-Cd alloy).\n                        let flux = phi[idx];\n                        let t = if flux > 0.0 {\n                            ((flux.ln() - log_min) / log_range)\n                                .clamp(0.0, 1.0)\n                        } else {\n                            0.0\n                        };\n                        let base = 80.0 + t * 40.0;\n                        ((base * 1.1) as u8, (base * 0.7) as u8, (base * 0.75) as u8)\n                    } else {\n                        // Water channel: light blue tint.\n                        let flux = phi[idx];\n                        let t = if flux > 0.0 {\n                            ((flux.ln() - log_min) / log_range)\n                                .clamp(0.0, 1.0)\n                        } else {\n                            0.0\n                        };\n                        let b = (40.0 + t * 100.0) as u8;\n                        (b / 4, b / 3, b)\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        30\n    }\n}\n",
   "cargo_toml": null
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_instruments",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "/// # Reactor Instrumentation: k_eff & Flux Time Plots\n///\n/// Reads `k_eff` and `total_flux` from the simulation bus and\n/// plots them as scrolling time-series graphs.\n\nconst W: u32 = 640;\nconst H: u32 = 300;\nconst HISTORY: usize = 600;  // data points (= canvas width in px)\nconst MARGIN_L: u32 = 50;\nconst MARGIN_R: u32 = 10;\nconst MARGIN_T: u32 = 25;\nconst MARGIN_B: u32 = 25;\n\nstruct Instruments {\n    k_history: Vec<f64>,\n    flux_history: Vec<f64>,\n    flux_peak: f64,\n    frame: u64,\n}\n\nimpl Simulation for Instruments {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        Self {\n            k_history: Vec::with_capacity(HISTORY),\n            flux_history: Vec::with_capacity(HISTORY),\n            flux_peak: 1.0,\n            frame: 0,\n        }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> Canvas {\n        self.frame += 1;\n\n        // Read from the sim bus (emitted by the reactor simulation).\n        let k = sim::read::<f64>(\"k_eff\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n        let flux = sim::read::<f64>(\"total_flux\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n\n        // Append to histories.\n        self.k_history.push(k);\n        self.flux_history.push(flux);\n        if self.k_history.len() > HISTORY {\n            self.k_history.remove(0);\n        }\n        if self.flux_history.len() > HISTORY {\n            self.flux_history.remove(0);\n        }\n\n        // Track peak flux for scaling (slow decay).\n        self.flux_peak = flux.max(self.flux_peak * 0.999);\n        let flux_ceil = self.flux_peak.max(1.0);\n\n        // \u2500\u2500 Render \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n        let plot_w = (W - MARGIN_L - MARGIN_R) as usize;\n        let half_h = H / 2;\n        let plot_h = (half_h - MARGIN_T - MARGIN_B) as usize;\n\n        let k_hist = &self.k_history;\n        let f_hist = &self.flux_history;\n\n        Canvas::from_fn(W, H, move |px, py| {\n            // Background.\n            let bg = (18, 18, 24);\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Top half: k_eff plot \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            if py < half_h {\n                let plot_y0 = MARGIN_T;\n                let plot_y1 = half_h - MARGIN_B;\n\n                // Axis labels area.\n                if px < MARGIN_L {\n                    // Draw \"k\" label and scale ticks via simple pixel art? Skip for now.\n                    return bg;\n                }\n                if px >= W - MARGIN_R {\n                    return bg;\n                }\n\n                let xi = (px - MARGIN_L) as usize;\n                let yi = py;\n\n                if yi < plot_y0 || yi >= plot_y1 {\n                    return bg;\n                }\n\n                let plot_yi = (yi - plot_y0) as usize;\n\n                // k_eff range: 0.0 to 2.0\n                let _k_at_y = 2.0 * (1.0 - plot_yi as f64 / plot_h as f64);\n\n                // Criticality line (k=1).\n                let k1_row = (plot_h as f64 * 0.5) as usize;\n                if plot_yi == k1_row {\n                    return (60, 60, 70);\n                }\n\n                // Plot the data point at this x.\n                if xi < k_hist.len() {\n                    let offset = if k_hist.len() > plot_w { k_hist.len() - plot_w } else { 0 };\n                    if xi < k_hist.len() - offset {\n                        let val = k_hist[xi + offset];\n                        let val_row = ((1.0 - val / 2.0) * plot_h as f64) as usize;\n                        let dist = if plot_yi >= val_row { plot_yi - val_row } else { val_row - plot_yi };\n                        if dist <= 1 {\n                            // Colour by k value.\n                            if val < 0.95 {\n                                return (80, 140, 255);      // subcritical: blue\n                            } else if val > 1.05 {\n                                return (255, 80, 80);       // supercritical: red\n                            } else {\n                                return (80, 255, 80);       // near-critical: green\n                            }\n                        }\n                    }\n                }\n\n                return bg;\n            }\n\n            // \u2500\u2500 Bottom half: total flux plot \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n            let lower_y = py - half_h;\n            let plot_y0 = MARGIN_T;\n            let plot_y1 = half_h - MARGIN_B;\n\n            if px < MARGIN_L || px >= W - MARGIN_R {\n                return bg;\n            }\n            if lower_y < plot_y0 || lower_y >= plot_y1 {\n                return bg;\n            }\n\n            let xi = (px - MARGIN_L) as usize;\n            let plot_yi = (lower_y - plot_y0) as usize;\n\n            // Flux range: 0 to flux_ceil (linear).\n            if xi < f_hist.len() {\n                let offset = if f_hist.len() > plot_w { f_hist.len() - plot_w } else { 0 };\n                if xi < f_hist.len() - offset {\n                    let val = f_hist[xi + offset];\n                    let frac = (val / flux_ceil).clamp(0.0, 1.0);\n                    let val_row = ((1.0 - frac) * plot_h as f64) as usize;\n                    let dist = if plot_yi >= val_row { plot_yi - val_row } else { val_row - plot_yi };\n                    if dist <= 1 {\n                        return (80, 220, 255);  // cyan\n                    }\n                }\n            }\n\n            bg\n        })\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        30\n    }\n}",
   "order": 13,
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Instruments"
  },
  {
   "id": "cell_dashboard",
   "cell_type": "Code",
   "source": "/// # Reactor Dashboard: LiveView\n///\n/// Real-time HTML dashboard showing reactor telemetry via the `LiveView` trait.\n/// Reads `k_eff` and `total_flux` from the simulation bus and renders a\n/// control-room-style status panel with gauges and status indicators.\n\nstruct Dashboard {\n    tick_count: u32,\n}\n\nimpl LiveView for Dashboard {\n    fn init() -> Self {\n        Self { tick_count: 0 }\n    }\n\n    fn tick(&mut self) -> LiveContent {\n        self.tick_count += 1;\n\n        let k_eff = sim::read::<f64>(\"k_eff\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n        let total_flux = sim::read::<f64>(\"total_flux\").unwrap_or(0.0);\n\n        // Determine reactor status and accent colour.\n        let (status, color, status_desc) = if k_eff < 0.95 {\n            (\"SUBCRITICAL\", \"#4fc3f7\", \"Chain reaction dying out\")\n        } else if k_eff > 1.05 {\n            (\"SUPERCRITICAL\", \"#ef5350\", \"Power excursion; loss of control in progress\")\n        } else {\n            (\"CRITICAL\", \"#66bb6a\", \"Steady-state fission\")\n        };\n\n        // k_eff gauge: map 0.5\u20131.5 \u2192 0\u2013100%\n        let k_pct = ((k_eff - 0.5) / 1.0).clamp(0.0, 1.0) * 100.0;\n\n        // Flux gauge: log-scale, map 1e0\u20131e12 \u2192 0\u2013100%.\n        let flux_pct = if total_flux > 1.0 {\n            (total_flux.log10() / 12.0).clamp(0.0, 1.0) * 100.0\n        } else {\n            0.0\n        };\n\n        let html = format!(\n            r#\"<div style=\"background:#12121c;color:#d4d4d4;padding:20px;border-radius:10px;font-family:'Courier New',monospace;max-width:520px;border:1px solid #2a2a3a;\">\n  <div style=\"display:flex;align-items:center;gap:10px;margin-bottom:16px;\">\n    <span style=\"font-size:22px;\">\u2622\ufe0f</span>\n    <span style=\"font-size:17px;font-weight:bold;color:#90caf9;letter-spacing:1px;\">REACTOR STATUS PANEL</span>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:14px;\">\n    <div style=\"background:#1a1a2e;padding:14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #2a2a3a;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;margin-bottom:6px;\">k_eff</div>\n      <div style=\"font-size:32px;font-weight:bold;color:{color};line-height:1;\">{k_eff:.5}</div>\n      <div style=\"background:#2a2a3a;border-radius:4px;height:8px;margin-top:8px;overflow:hidden;\">\n        <div style=\"background:{color};height:100%;width:{k_pct:.1}%;border-radius:4px;transition:width 0.1s;\"></div>\n      </div>\n      <div style=\"font-size:10px;color:#666;margin-top:4px;\">0.50 \u2500\u2500 1.00 \u2500\u2500 1.50</div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"background:#1a1a2e;padding:14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #2a2a3a;\">\n      <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;margin-bottom:6px;\">Status</div>\n      <div style=\"font-size:22px;font-weight:bold;color:{color};line-height:1;\">{status}</div>\n      <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;margin-top:8px;\">{status_desc}</div>\n    </div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"background:#1a1a2e;padding:14px;border-radius:8px;border:1px solid #2a2a3a;margin-top:14px;\">\n    <div style=\"font-size:11px;color:#888;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:1px;margin-bottom:6px;\">Total Neutron Flux</div>\n    <div style=\"font-size:26px;font-weight:bold;color:#ce93d8;line-height:1;\">{total_flux:.3e}<span style=\"font-size:13px;color:#888;margin-left:6px;\">n\u00b7cm\u207b\u00b2\u00b7s\u207b\u00b9</span></div>\n    <div style=\"background:#2a2a3a;border-radius:4px;height:8px;margin-top:8px;overflow:hidden;\">\n      <div style=\"background:#ce93d8;height:100%;width:{flux_pct:.1}%;border-radius:4px;transition:width 0.1s;\"></div>\n    </div>\n    <div style=\"font-size:10px;color:#666;margin-top:4px;\">log\u2081\u2080 scale: 10\u2070 \u2500\u2500 10\u2076 \u2500\u2500 10\u00b9\u00b2</div>\n  </div>\n  <div style=\"margin-top:12px;font-size:10px;color:#555;text-align:right;\">tick #{tick_count}</div>\n</div>\"#,\n            tick_count = self.tick_count,\n        );\n\n        LiveContent::Html(html)\n    }\n\n    fn fps() -> u32 {\n        10\n    }\n}",
   "order": 14,
   "cargo_toml": null,
   "label": "Dashboard"
  }
 ],
 "description": "2D neutron diffusion with 6-group delayed precursors: watch prompt vs delayed neutrons pulse through a reactor core.",
 "tags": [
  "physics",
  "simulation",
  "nuclear",
  "pde",
  "live-simulation",
  "blog"
 ]
}
